// src/kernel/core/shell/functor_invoke.pdx -- UMB-W5 R123.M3 (paideia-os #2587)
//
// In-process functor invoke ABI. Sits on top of the UMB-W1 R123.M1
// KIND_CMD_MODULE substrate (src/kernel/core/cap/kind_cmd_module.pdx,
// kind tag 0x5C, 8 rows x 48 bytes). Frozen ABI for the shell -> loaded
// functor call path in the SAME address space (ring-0 kernel today;
// R123.M4 adds the out-of-process spawn-based path for untrusted
// functors).
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0  WHAT R123.M3 LANDS vs. WHAT A FOLLOW-UP OWNS
// =============================================================================
//
// LANDED HERE (R123.M3):
//
//   (1) The three ABI primitives named by the parent task directive:
//
//         _functor_invoke_lookup(name_ptr, name_len) -> u64
//         _functor_invoke_call(functor_entry_va, argv_ptr, argc) -> i64
//         _functor_invoke_check_cap(name_ptr, name_len, caller_cap_env)
//                                                               -> u64
//
//   (2) A parallel shadow table `_fi_entry_va : [u64; 8]`, indexed 1:1
//       by KIND_CMD_MODULE row_id. Lookup resolves name -> row_id via
//       kcm_find_hash (the substrate's own hash scan) and then reads
//       the row_id's entry in this shadow. We parallel-index rather
//       than reinterpret `_kcm_table`[row+40] because the substrate
//       already pre-commits that word to `cap_env_ptr` once R125.M1
//       cap-env integration lands (see kind_cmd_module.pdx §2); using
//       a shadow avoids the R125 reconciliation.
//
//   (3) `_functor_invoke_bind(name_ptr, name_len, functor_entry_va)`
//       -- the write side of the shadow. Not part of the three-API
//       surface the parent spec names, but needed so a follow-up
//       (R123.M3-followup: static-builtin binding) can populate the
//       shadow once builtin functor addresses are known. Kernel-trusted
//       entry; no cap-invoke exposure.
//
// DEFERRED (follow-up issues):
//
//   (a) Shell-dispatch wiring. There is NO existing shell_main /
//       shell_dispatch loop in src/kernel/core/shell/ today (grep
//       audit, 2026-10-11): the three files under shell/ are
//       cmd_registry.pdx (LRU cache + reload), cmd_registry_parser.pdx
//       (TOML -> KIND_CMD_MODULE rows), and cmd_invoke.pdx (R123.M3-
//       light-cmd fast path for the earlier 0x200 substrate; dispatched
//       from the boot witness only). The parent task's §3 pre-hook
//       cannot land here because no caller exists to hook. Deferred to
//       the shell-dispatch wiring landing that follows R123.M4.
//
//   (b) Real cap-env rights check. `_functor_invoke_check_cap` is a
//       SKELETON: it validates that the name resolves (so the
//       NOT_FOUND code is testable) and otherwise returns FI_OK. The
//       real check -- "does caller_cap_env_cap grant the functor's
//       declared `capability` field?" -- requires the cap-env rows
//       to carry a reverse-index of capability labels, which R125
//       integration lands.
//
//   (c) Multi-shadow for per-process functor tables. _fi_entry_va is
//       a kernel-global shadow for M3. Per-process functor tables
//       (so a userspace process can bind its own functors into its
//       own shell) land post-ring-3-materialisation.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 1  WHY TOUCH THE KIND_CMD_MODULE SUBSTRATE AT ALL
// =============================================================================
//
// The parent spec names three APIs that:
//
//   * take a NAME (lookup, check_cap), OR a raw VA (call),
//   * and must agree on what "the same functor" means across these.
//
// If lookup held its own name->VA store independent of the capability
// substrate, two issues follow: (i) a cap-invoke caller holding a
// KIND_CMD_MODULE handle and the shell holding a lookup entry would be
// looking at two different name-indexed records -- a capability model
// hazard; (ii) the parse-time registry population (R123.M2) that
// mints the substrate rows would need a second write path. Both drop
// away by anchoring lookup on the substrate's own name_hash scan.
//
// _fi_entry_va[row_id] is strictly a shadow of the substrate: it is
// zero whenever _kcm_table[row_id] is EMPTY or no bind has yet
// populated it, and non-zero exactly when a functor address is known.
// The two tables are kept consistent by the discipline that
// _functor_invoke_bind refuses to populate a dead row, and the
// substrate's `cmd_module_close` currently does NOT scrub the shadow
// (M3-known deferral -- the shadow is cleared defensively at boot
// via fi_shadow_reset and never cleared on close; the next
// cmd_module_open to the same row_id will mint a zero flags word per
// publish-last and the next bind replaces the stale shadow entry).
//
// A close + re-open against the same row_id without a bind would
// leave a stale functor VA readable. R123.M3-followup (shell-dispatch
// wiring) adds the close-side scrub by invoking fi_scrub_row(row_id)
// in cmd_module_close's post-publish path; today it is a known gap,
// documented at design/shell/functor-invoke-abi.md §Gaps.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 2  ABI
// =============================================================================
//
// SysV AMD64, matches paideia-as calling convention.
//
//   _functor_invoke_lookup(name_ptr: u64, name_len: u64) -> u64
//     rdi = name_ptr
//     rsi = name_len
//     rax = (functor_entry_va << 1) | 1  on hit
//     rax = 0                              on miss
//     Low bit encodes found-ness; VA bit 0 is always 0 because every
//     functor entry sits on an even instruction boundary (x86_64 does
//     not require it architecturally, but every paideia-as emit lands
//     on at least 1-byte alignment and the kernel's own function
//     symbols are all even-addressed in practice -- see design doc
//     §VA-tag for the audit). A bad input (name_ptr == 0, name_len
//     not in [1..64]) returns 0 (indistinguishable from miss by
//     design: lookup is a query, not a validator; check_cap is the
//     validation surface).
//
//   _functor_invoke_call(functor_entry_va: u64, argv_ptr: u64,
//                        argc: u64) -> i64
//     rdi = functor_entry_va (RAW VA, NOT the packed-tag form)
//     rsi = argv_ptr
//     rdx = argc
//     rax = functor's i64 return (shell exit code)
//     The call sets up a fresh frame (push rbp; mov rbp, rsp) so a
//     frame-walker sees the shell -> functor boundary. SysV callee-
//     saves (rbx, rbp, r12..r15) are preserved by this file's own
//     prologue/epilogue; the functor's callee-save discipline is its
//     own concern. The caller remains responsible for the i64 return
//     convention: a shell-exit-code value in [0..255], or a signed
//     negative to signal an abnormal termination (R123.M4 lands the
//     wait-shaped translation).
//     A zero or odd functor_entry_va is NOT re-validated here (caller
//     must have already filtered via _functor_invoke_lookup); a bad
//     VA faults through #PF or #UD, which the kernel's trap handlers
//     surface as the usual death-path audit.
//
//   _functor_invoke_check_cap(name_ptr: u64, name_len: u64,
//                             caller_cap_env_cap: u64) -> u64
//     rdi = name_ptr
//     rsi = name_len
//     rdx = caller_cap_env_cap (opaque; a KIND_CAP_ENV handle or 0)
//     rax = FI_OK (0) on allow
//     rax = FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND if the name does not resolve
//     rax = FI_ERR_CAP_DENIED (reserved, never raised at M3)
//     rax = FI_ERR_BAD_ARGV (name shape invalid)
//     At M3: ONLY the shape + name-resolution gates fire; the
//     capability narrowing is a stub. R125 integration flips the stub
//     body to the real reverse-index check.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 3  FAILURE TAXONOMY -- 0x0C21..0x0C23
// =============================================================================
//
// Band audit (2026-10-11): cmd_registry_parser.pdx §2 claims 0x0C01..
// 0x0C04. The gap 0x0C05..0x0C1F is unclaimed (grep over
// `0x0C[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}` returned those four values and nothing else in
// the band). This file lands at 0x0C21..0x0C23 to leave the 0x0C10
// strip clear for any R123.M4 out-of-process invoke that wants a
// contiguous band adjacent to CRP's. All three fit imm32 (zero-extend
// to u64 via the `mov rax, imm32` 7-byte emit, same posture as
// kind_cmd_module.pdx §5 and cmd_registry_parser.pdx §2).
//
//   FI_OK              = 0x0000
//   FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND   = 0x0C21  (name not registered in the shadow,
//                                  or the row's flags word says EMPTY
//                                  (never-opened or closed), or bind
//                                  has not populated the shadow slot)
//   FI_ERR_CAP_DENIED  = 0x0C22  (M3 placeholder: the R125 cap-env
//                                  integration will raise this when
//                                  the functor's declared capability
//                                  is not in caller_cap_env_cap's
//                                  rights bitmap)
//   FI_ERR_BAD_ARGV    = 0x0C23  (name_ptr == 0, name_len == 0, or
//                                  name_len > 64; also used by bind
//                                  for the same shape violations)
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 4  ENCODER DISCIPLINE
// =============================================================================
//
// paideia-as encoder posture (feedback_pdx_encoder_pitfalls):
//   * Every zero-check uses `cmp reg, 0` (never `test rN, rN`).
//   * No `and reg, imm64` -- 0xFFFFFFFF stages through r10/r11.
//   * No 2-op `imul r, imm` -- shadow table stride 8 uses `shl 3`.
//   * No `cmp reg, [mem]` -- every memory operand lands in a register
//     first (load-into-r10 then compare).
//   * No `mov_b [mem]` byte-store (not needed here).
//   * Reserved-label prefix `fi_` (short for functor_invoke) avoids
//     the `loop`/`if`/`top`/`done` collision class per
//     feedback_paideia_as_reserved_labels. Inner labels carry a
//     second-level prefix (fi_lu_ lookup, fi_cl_ call, fi_cc_
//     check_cap, fi_bn_ bind, fi_sr_ shadow-reset, fi_sx_ scrub-row,
//     fi_nt_ note).
//   * Module basename `FunctorInvoke` matches the file basename
//     `functor_invoke.pdx`.
//   * All `pub let` string / byte-array declarations are single-line.
//   * Fingerprint tag `[u8; N]` byte-array literals have N = string
//     length + 1 (the trailing NUL counts).
//
// klog_s1_x1 convention: key in rcx, value in r8; no stack arg.
// klog_s1_x2 convention: k1 rcx, v1 r8, k2 r9, v2 at caller `[rsp+0]`
// after `sub rsp,16`. This file uses klog_s1_x2 for the LOOKUP and
// BIND fingerprints (two hex KVs) and klog_s1_x1 for CALL and CHECK
// (one hex KV).

module FunctorInvoke = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 5  CONSTANTS
  // ==========================================================================

  // Return codes (§3).
  pub let FI_OK              : u64 = 0
  pub let FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND   : u64 = 0x0C21
  pub let FI_ERR_CAP_DENIED  : u64 = 0x0C22
  pub let FI_ERR_BAD_ARGV    : u64 = 0x0C23

  // Row-pool ceilings mirrored from kind_cmd_module.pdx (local copies
  // so this module can fold them without pulling the KindCmdModule
  // namespace into every call site, same posture as cmd_registry_
  // parser.pdx CRP_MAX_ROWS / CRP_NAME_MAX).
  pub let FI_ROW_MAX  : u64 = 8     // mirrors CM_MODULE_MAX
  pub let FI_NAME_MAX : u64 = 64    // mirrors CM_NAME_MAX

  // Substrate-side sentinels we fold against.
  pub let FI_KCM_ROW_NONE : u64 = 0xFF   // mirrors kind_cmd_module CM_ROW_NONE

  // Lookup packed-tag masks (§2).
  pub let FI_PACK_HIT_BIT : u64 = 0x1

  // Observability counters.
  pub let mut _fi_stats : [u64; 8] = uninit @align(64)

  pub let FI_ST_LOOKUP_HIT   : u64 = 0
  pub let FI_ST_LOOKUP_MISS  : u64 = 1
  pub let FI_ST_LOOKUP_BAD   : u64 = 2
  pub let FI_ST_CALL_OK      : u64 = 3
  pub let FI_ST_CHECK_OK     : u64 = 4
  pub let FI_ST_CHECK_MISS   : u64 = 5
  pub let FI_ST_BIND_OK      : u64 = 6
  pub let FI_ST_BIND_MISS    : u64 = 7

  // Shadow table (§1). Parallel-indexed by KIND_CMD_MODULE row_id.
  // _fi_entry_va[row_id] == 0 encodes "no functor bound at row_id",
  // which collapses with the EMPTY-row case into "no functor known".
  // Both cases return 0 from _functor_invoke_lookup.
  pub let mut _fi_entry_va : [u64; 8] = uninit @align(64)

  // Fingerprint tags + keys. Byte counts NUL-inclusive per the
  // kernel-logging-substrate.md #699 discipline.
  pub let fi_tag_lookup : [u8; 15] = "FUNCTOR LOOKUP\0"
  pub let fi_tag_call   : [u8; 13] = "FUNCTOR CALL\0"
  pub let fi_tag_check  : [u8; 14] = "FUNCTOR CHECK\0"
  pub let fi_tag_bind   : [u8; 13] = "FUNCTOR BIND\0"

  pub let fi_k_name_hash : [u8; 10] = "name_hash\0"
  pub let fi_k_va        : [u8; 3]  = "va\0"
  pub let fi_k_result    : [u8; 7]  = "result\0"
  pub let fi_k_rowid     : [u8; 6]  = "rowid\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 6  fi_shadow_reset() -> ()
  //
  // Defensive clear. Zero the shadow table (8 u64) and the stats
  // array (8 u64). .bss is already zero at real boot; this call is
  // belt-and-braces per the kind_cmd_module.pdx :: kcm_table_reset
  // precedent, callable from kernel_main's derived-kind reset
  // cascade (adjacent to kcm_table_reset).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let fi_shadow_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W5 R123.M3 (#2587): clear every _fi_entry_va u64 (8 words) and every _fi_stats u64 (8 words). Boot + teardown only. .bss already zero at real boot; this is defensive (matches kcm_table_reset posture one-for-one). Labels prefixed fi_sr_.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _fi_entry_va];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;
      fi_sr_shadow:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 8;
        jb  fi_sr_shadow;

        lea r10, [rip + _fi_stats];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      fi_sr_stats:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 8;
        jb  fi_sr_stats;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 7  fi_note(slot) -- bounded stats increment.
  //
  // Byte-identical to kcm_note / cr_note. Caller passes slot ordinal
  // (FI_ST_*). Load-inc-store idiom (no `add reg, [mem]`).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let fi_note : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W5 R123.M3 (#2587): bump _fi_stats[slot] by 1. Slot ordinals in {0..7} per FI_ST_* enumeration. Pure leaf, bounds-checked. Labels prefixed fi_nt_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 8;
        jae fi_nt_out;
        lea r10, [rip + _fi_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + rdi * 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + rdi * 8], rax;
      fi_nt_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 8  fi_scrub_row(row_id) -> ()
  //
  // Zero _fi_entry_va[row_id]. Idempotent; bounds-checked. Callers:
  // the shell-dispatch wiring landing (post-cmd_module_close) and
  // _functor_invoke_bind on the failure path.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let fi_scrub_row : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W5 R123.M3 (#2587): zero _fi_entry_va[row_id] after a cmd_module_close so a stale functor VA cannot be read via a re-opened row_id. Bounded by FI_ROW_MAX=8. Labels prefixed fi_sx_.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 8;                      // FI_ROW_MAX
        cmp rdi, r10;
        jae fi_sx_out;
        lea r10, [rip + _fi_entry_va];
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r10 + rdi * 8], rax;
      fi_sx_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 9  _functor_invoke_lookup(name_ptr, name_len) -> u64
  //
  // Pipeline:
  //   (1) Validate: name_ptr != 0, 0 < name_len <= FI_NAME_MAX.
  //       Bad shape: bump FI_ST_LOOKUP_BAD, return 0 (miss sentinel).
  //       The lookup surface is a QUERY; validation is check_cap's job.
  //   (2) Hash via kcm_name_hash (substrate's own djb2 variant).
  //   (3) Scan via kcm_find_hash. Miss (returns FI_KCM_ROW_NONE 0xFF):
  //       bump FI_ST_LOOKUP_MISS, return 0.
  //   (4) Load _fi_entry_va[row_id]. Zero means "no functor bound to
  //       this row yet" -- fold into the miss path.
  //   (5) Emit `FUNCTOR LOOKUP name_hash=0x<...> result=0x<packed>`
  //       via klog_s1_x2.
  //   (6) Bump FI_ST_LOOKUP_HIT; return (va << 1) | 1.
  //
  // Register plan: four callee-save pushes (rbx = name_ptr, r12 =
  // name_len, r13 = name_hash, r14 = va then packed-result) + sub
  // rsp,8 land rsp%16==0 at nested calls (entry 8 + 32 + 8 = 48,
  // 48%16==0). klog_s1_x2 caller frame uses sub rsp,16 (v2 at
  // [rsp+0] per Wave 73 I7 regression).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _functor_invoke_lookup : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{} =
    fn (name_ptr: u64) (name_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W5 R123.M3 (#2587): resolve a shell command name to a packed functor_entry_va. Pipeline: (1) validate shape (name_ptr!=0 AND 1<=name_len<=64); bad shape bumps FI_ST_LOOKUP_BAD and returns 0 (miss sentinel, by design -- lookup is a query, not a validator); (2) kcm_name_hash djb2; (3) kcm_find_hash row scan; miss returns 0; (4) read _fi_entry_va[row_id]; zero folds into miss; (5) emit FUNCTOR LOOKUP fingerprint via klog_s1_x2 (hex name_hash + hex result); (6) return (va << 1) | 1. Low-bit encoding keeps VA bit 0 (always 0 on even-aligned entries, see §VA-tag in design doc). Register plan: four callee-save pushes (rbx=name_ptr, r12=name_len, r13=name_hash, r14=va/packed) + sub rsp,8 (entry 8 + 32 + 8 = 48, 48%16==0 at nested calls). klog_s1_x2 caller frame uses sub rsp,16 (v2 at [rsp+0]). Effects widened to {sysreg,mem} because the fingerprint emit path touches uart via klog_emit_core. Labels prefixed fi_lu_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        sub  rsp, 8;

        mov rbx, rdi;                    // name_ptr
        mov r12, rsi;                    // name_len

        // (1) Shape gate.
        cmp rbx, 0;
        je  fi_lu_bad;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je  fi_lu_bad;
        mov r10, 64;                     // FI_NAME_MAX
        cmp r12, r10;
        ja  fi_lu_bad;

        // (2) Hash.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call kcm_name_hash;
        mov r13, rax;                    // name_hash

        // (3) Row scan.
        mov rdi, r13;
        call kcm_find_hash;
        mov r10, 0xFF;                   // FI_KCM_ROW_NONE
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  fi_lu_miss;

        // rax = row_id in [0..7].
        // (4) Shadow load.
        lea r10, [rip + _fi_entry_va];
        mov r14, [r10 + rax * 8];        // r14 = va (0 if unbound)
        cmp r14, 0;
        je  fi_lu_miss;

        // Pack result = (va << 1) | 1. Reuse r14 (va is done after
        // this; the pack is a pure transform).
        mov rax, r14;
        shl rax, 1;
        mov r10, 1;                      // FI_PACK_HIT_BIT
        or  rax, r10;
        mov r14, rax;                    // r14 = packed result

        // (5) Fingerprint.
        sub rsp, 16;
        mov [rsp + 0], r14;              // v2 = packed result
        mov rdi, 3;                      // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_CAP_];
        lea rdx, [rip + fi_tag_lookup];
        lea rcx, [rip + fi_k_name_hash]; // k1
        mov r8,  r13;                    // v1 = name_hash
        lea r9,  [rip + fi_k_result];    // k2
        call klog_s1_x2;
        add rsp, 16;

        // (6) Bump + return.
        mov rdi, 0;                      // FI_ST_LOOKUP_HIT
        call fi_note;

        mov rax, r14;                    // packed result
        jmp fi_lu_out;

      fi_lu_bad:
        mov rdi, 2;                      // FI_ST_LOOKUP_BAD
        call fi_note;
        xor rax, rax;
        jmp fi_lu_out;

      fi_lu_miss:
        mov rdi, 1;                      // FI_ST_LOOKUP_MISS
        call fi_note;
        xor rax, rax;

      fi_lu_out:
        add rsp, 8;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 10  _functor_invoke_call(functor_entry_va, argv_ptr, argc)
  //                                                              -> i64
  //
  // In-process indirect call. Preserves SysV callee-saves, sets up a
  // fresh frame (push rbp / mov rbp, rsp), and dispatches via
  // `call rN` (register-indirect: the VA is runtime data, not a
  // static symbol).
  //
  // Register trap: klog_s1_x1 clobbers rsi + rdx. Spill those on the
  // frame BEFORE the fingerprint emit; reload AFTER into the SysV
  // positions the functor expects (argv_ptr -> rdi, argc -> rsi).
  //
  // Frame layout under rbp (sub rsp,40 = 32 frame + 8 pad):
  //   [rbp - 8]   argv_ptr spill
  //   [rbp - 16]  argc spill
  //   [rbp - 24]  va stash
  //   [rbp - 32]  reserved (callee-save alignment pad)
  //
  // Register plan: five callee-save pushes (rbx = va mirror, r12..r15
  // for alignment symmetry) + push rbp + sub rsp,40 land rsp%16==0
  // at every nested call. Alignment math:
  //   entry      rsp%16 == 8
  //   push rbx                  8  -> 0
  //   push r12                  8  -> 8
  //   push r13                  8  -> 0
  //   push r14                  8  -> 8
  //   push r15                  8  -> 0
  //   push rbp                  8  -> 8
  //   sub  rsp, 40             40  -> 0  (at nested calls)
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _functor_invoke_call : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{} =
    fn (functor_entry_va: u64) (argv_ptr: u64) (argc: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W5 R123.M3 (#2587): in-process indirect call to a bound shell functor. Preserves SysV callee-saves (rbx + r12..r15 pushed; rbp pushed inside the fresh frame so a stack-walker sees exactly one shell->functor boundary). Spill argv_ptr/argc BEFORE klog_s1_x1 (which clobbers rsi/rdx), reload after. Functor sees (rdi=argv_ptr, rsi=argc) with the shell's callee-saves already saved HERE; return value passes through verbatim in rax (i64 convention). VA is NOT re-validated: caller filtered via _functor_invoke_lookup; a bad VA faults through #PF or #UD. Frame layout under rbp: [rbp-8]=argv_ptr, [rbp-16]=argc, [rbp-24]=va, [rbp-32]=pad. Alignment: entry 8 + 5*8 + 8 (rbp) = 56 (%16==8); +40 (frame) = 96 (%16==0). FI_ST_CALL_OK bumps on return (counts 'the call returned without faulting'; the functor's semantic success is its own return code). Effects widened to {sysreg,mem} because the fingerprint emit touches uart. Labels prefixed fi_cl_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        push rbp;
        mov  rbp, rsp;
        sub  rsp, 40;                    // 32 frame + 8 pad

        // Spill the three ABI args into the frame BEFORE klog.
        mov [rbp - 8],  rsi;             // argv_ptr spill
        mov [rbp - 16], rdx;             // argc spill
        mov [rbp - 24], rdi;             // va stash
        mov rbx, rdi;                    // va also in rbx (survives
                                         //   nested call clobbers)

        // Fingerprint: FUNCTOR CALL va=<...> via klog_s1_x1.
        mov rdi, 3;                      // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_CAP_];
        lea rdx, [rip + fi_tag_call];
        lea rcx, [rip + fi_k_va];        // k1
        mov r8,  rbx;                    // v1 = va
        call klog_s1_x1;

        // Reload the functor's SysV args. Functor signature:
        //   fn(argv_ptr, argc) -> i64
        // so arg1 = rdi = argv_ptr, arg2 = rsi = argc.
        mov rdi, [rbp - 8];              // argv_ptr
        mov rsi, [rbp - 16];             // argc

        // Indirect call via register.
        mov rax, [rbp - 24];             // va
        call rax;
                                         // rax = functor's return

        // Stash functor return across the FI_ST_CALL_OK bump.
        mov r12, rax;

        mov rdi, 3;                      // FI_ST_CALL_OK
        call fi_note;

        mov rax, r12;                    // restore functor return

        add rsp, 40;
        pop rbp;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 11  _functor_invoke_check_cap(name_ptr, name_len,
  //                                        caller_cap_env_cap) -> u64
  //
  // SKELETON for R123.M3. Validates the name shape and resolves it
  // via _functor_invoke_lookup to prove the name is bound. The real
  // capability narrowing (does caller_cap_env_cap grant the functor's
  // declared `capability`?) is DEFERRED to the R125 cap-env
  // integration M-step, which replaces the stub body with the reverse-
  // index check.
  //
  // Returns:
  //   FI_OK              -- shape valid AND name resolves. M3 stub
  //                          treats this as "capability granted".
  //   FI_ERR_BAD_ARGV    -- name_ptr == 0, name_len == 0, or
  //                          name_len > 64.
  //   FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND   -- shape valid but name does not resolve.
  //   FI_ERR_CAP_DENIED  -- RESERVED. R125 integration raises this.
  //
  // Register plan: three callee-save pushes (rbx = name_ptr, r12 =
  // name_len, r13 = caller_cap_env_cap) + sub rsp,16 land rsp%16==0
  // at nested calls (entry 8 + 24 + 16 = 48, 48%16==0).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _functor_invoke_check_cap : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{} =
    fn (name_ptr: u64) (name_len: u64) (caller_cap_env_cap: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W5 R123.M3 (#2587) SKELETON: validate name shape (name_ptr!=0 AND 1<=name_len<=64) and prove it resolves via _functor_invoke_lookup. Returns FI_OK on resolve, FI_ERR_BAD_ARGV on bad shape, FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND on resolve miss. caller_cap_env_cap is accepted but NOT checked at M3 -- the real reverse-index check lands in R125 cap-env integration (where the KIND_CAP_ENV rows carry a capability-label index). Emits FUNCTOR CHECK result fingerprint via klog_s1_x1 on each exit path. Register plan: three callee-save pushes (rbx=name_ptr, r12=name_len, r13=caller_cap_env_cap) + sub rsp,16 land rsp%16==0 at nested calls (entry 8 + 24 + 16 = 48, 48%16==0). Labels prefixed fi_cc_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        sub  rsp, 16;

        mov rbx, rdi;                    // name_ptr
        mov r12, rsi;                    // name_len
        mov r13, rdx;                    // caller_cap_env_cap (unused M3)

        // Shape gate.
        cmp rbx, 0;
        je  fi_cc_bad;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je  fi_cc_bad;
        mov r10, 64;                     // FI_NAME_MAX
        cmp r12, r10;
        ja  fi_cc_bad;

        // Resolve via lookup.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call _functor_invoke_lookup;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  fi_cc_miss;

        // Hit. M3 stub treats resolve as cap-granted.
        mov rdi, 4;                      // FI_ST_CHECK_OK
        call fi_note;

        // Fingerprint: result = FI_OK.
        mov rdi, 3;                      // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_CAP_];
        lea rdx, [rip + fi_tag_check];
        lea rcx, [rip + fi_k_result];
        xor r8,  r8;                     // v1 = FI_OK (0)
        call klog_s1_x1;

        xor rax, rax;                    // FI_OK
        jmp fi_cc_out;

      fi_cc_miss:
        mov rdi, 5;                      // FI_ST_CHECK_MISS
        call fi_note;

        mov rdi, 3;                      // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_CAP_];
        lea rdx, [rip + fi_tag_check];
        lea rcx, [rip + fi_k_result];
        mov r8,  0x0C21;                 // FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND
        call klog_s1_x1;

        mov rax, 0x0C21;                 // FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND
        jmp fi_cc_out;

      fi_cc_bad:
        mov rax, 0x0C23;                 // FI_ERR_BAD_ARGV

      fi_cc_out:
        add rsp, 16;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 12  _functor_invoke_bind(name_ptr, name_len,
  //                                   functor_entry_va) -> u64
  //
  // Write side of the shadow. Kernel-trusted; NOT part of the three-
  // API surface in §Spec but needed so the R123.M3-followup landing
  // (static-builtin binding) can populate _fi_entry_va once the
  // builtin functor addresses are known. The parser side
  // (cmd_registry_parser.pdx) is NOT the caller: the parser only
  // mints KIND_CMD_MODULE rows; the resolver that turns a
  // "pdx.builtin.<name>" functor key into a VA is a later landing.
  //
  // Pipeline:
  //   (1) Validate shape (same as lookup).
  //   (2) Validate va: must be non-zero AND 2-byte aligned (va & 1 == 0).
  //       Odd VA fails FI_ERR_BAD_ARGV because the packed-tag scheme
  //       in _functor_invoke_lookup would alias with the hit flag.
  //   (3) Hash + kcm_find_hash. Miss returns FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND.
  //   (4) Write _fi_entry_va[row_id] = va.
  //   (5) Emit `FUNCTOR BIND rowid=<n> va=<...>` via klog_s1_x2.
  //   (6) Bump FI_ST_BIND_OK.
  //
  // Register plan: five callee-save pushes (rbx = name_ptr, r12 =
  // name_len, r13 = va, r14 = row_id, r15 = pad). Entry rsp%16==8;
  // +40 -> %16==0 at nested calls.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _functor_invoke_bind : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{} =
    fn (name_ptr: u64) (name_len: u64) (functor_entry_va: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "UMB-W5 R123.M3 (#2587): populate _fi_entry_va[row_id] with a functor VA for the named module. Pipeline: (1) validate name shape; (2) va non-zero AND 2-byte aligned (odd VA fails because packed-tag scheme in _functor_invoke_lookup aliases the hit flag); (3) kcm_name_hash + kcm_find_hash; miss returns FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND; (4) write shadow slot; (5) emit FUNCTOR BIND rowid + va fingerprint via klog_s1_x2 (hex rowid + hex va); (6) bump FI_ST_BIND_OK. Register plan: five callee-save pushes (rbx=name_ptr, r12=name_len, r13=va, r14=row_id, r15=pad) leave rsp%16==0 at nested calls (entry 8 + 40 = 48, 48%16==0). klog_s1_x2 caller frame uses sub rsp,16 (v2 at [rsp+0]). Not part of the three-API surface in §Spec but needed by the R123.M3-followup static-builtin binding landing. Effects widened to {sysreg,mem} because the fingerprint emit touches uart. Labels prefixed fi_bn_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov rbx, rdi;                    // name_ptr
        mov r12, rsi;                    // name_len
        mov r13, rdx;                    // va

        // (1) Shape.
        cmp rbx, 0;
        je  fi_bn_bad;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je  fi_bn_bad;
        mov r10, 64;                     // FI_NAME_MAX
        cmp r12, r10;
        ja  fi_bn_bad;

        // (2) VA.
        cmp r13, 0;
        je  fi_bn_bad;
        mov r10, r13;
        mov r11, 1;
        and r10, r11;                    // va & 1
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne fi_bn_bad;                   // odd VA

        // (3) Hash + find.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call kcm_name_hash;

        mov rdi, rax;
        call kcm_find_hash;
        mov r10, 0xFF;                   // FI_KCM_ROW_NONE
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  fi_bn_miss;
        mov r14, rax;                    // row_id

        // (4) Write shadow.
        lea r10, [rip + _fi_entry_va];
        mov [r10 + r14 * 8], r13;

        // (5) Fingerprint.
        sub rsp, 16;
        mov [rsp + 0], r13;              // v2 = va
        mov rdi, 3;                      // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_CAP_];
        lea rdx, [rip + fi_tag_bind];
        lea rcx, [rip + fi_k_rowid];
        mov r8,  r14;                    // v1 = row_id
        lea r9,  [rip + fi_k_va];
        call klog_s1_x2;
        add rsp, 16;

        // (6) Bump + return.
        mov rdi, 6;                      // FI_ST_BIND_OK
        call fi_note;

        xor rax, rax;                    // FI_OK
        jmp fi_bn_out;

      fi_bn_miss:
        mov rdi, 7;                      // FI_ST_BIND_MISS
        call fi_note;
        mov rax, 0x0C21;                 // FI_ERR_NOT_FOUND
        jmp fi_bn_out;

      fi_bn_bad:
        mov rax, 0x0C23;                 // FI_ERR_BAD_ARGV

      fi_bn_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 13  Boot wiring note
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // kernel_main.pdx needs one new call alongside the existing cap/
  // substrate resets, placed adjacent to kcm_table_reset:
  //
  //   call fi_shadow_reset;             // defensive zero
  //
  // Zero-arg, allocates nothing on the stack, touches rax/rcx/r10;
  // safe alongside kernel_main's live handoff registers. Wiring
  // deferred to the next slice. At real boot .bss zero-init already
  // leaves _fi_entry_va empty, so lookup correctly MISSES for every
  // name until a bind call runs.
  //
  // Cross-module caller sites (future landings):
  //   - src/kernel/core/shell/shell_dispatch.pdx (R123.M4-adjacent):
  //     the shell's name-resolution pre-hook calls _functor_invoke_
  //     lookup; on hit calls _functor_invoke_call; on miss falls
  //     through to the builtin table (which does not yet exist in a
  //     dispatcher-shaped loop; the current `cmd_invoke_inproc` is a
  //     boot-witness callable, not a shell loop).
  //   - src/kernel/core/shell/cmd_registry_parser.pdx (follow-up
  //     landing): extends the per-section action to resolve the
  //     functor= value against a static-builtin table and call
  //     _functor_invoke_bind for each entry.
  //   - src/kernel/core/cap/kind_cmd_module.pdx :: cmd_module_close
  //     (follow-up landing): post-publish-last of flags := 0, calls
  //     fi_scrub_row(row_id) so a close+reopen without re-bind
  //     cannot inherit a stale VA.
  // ==========================================================================
}
